Below is a minimal, self‑contained example of how you could request **only the object IDs and their contexts** for the historical WHOIS records of `domain6.xyz`, capped at 8 results and using the `key_def` flag.  
(Replace the placeholder endpoint and authentication details with your own.)

```json
{
  "endpoint": "https://api.yourwhoisprovider.com/v1/historical-whois",
  "method": "GET",
  "headers": {
    "Authorization": "Bearer YOUR_API_TOKEN",
    "Accept": "application/json"
  },
  "params": {
    "domain": "domain6.xyz",
    "fields": "object_id,context",
    "limit": 8,
    "key_def": true
  }
}
```

### What the response will look like

```json
{
  "results": [
    {
      "object_id": "obj-00123",
      "context": "Registrar transfer – 2023-04-12"
    },
    {
      "object_id": "obj-00456",
      "context": "Name server change – 2022-11-08"
    },
    {
      "object_id": "obj-00789",
      "context": "Contact update – 2022-07-15"
    },
    {
      "object_id": "obj-01012",
      "context": "Domain renewal – 2021-12-01"
    },
    {
      "object_id": "obj-01345",
      "context": "Registrar transfer – 2021-06-30"
    },
    {
      "object_id": "obj-01678",
      "context": "Name server change – 2020-09-20"
    },
    {
      "object_id": "obj-01901",
      "context": "Contact update – 2020-03-05"
    },
    {
      "object_id": "obj-02234",
      "context": "Domain creation – 2019-01-10"
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "total": 12,
    "returned": 8,
    "limit": 8
  }
}
```

### Quick notes

| Parameter | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| `domain` | The domain you’re querying (`domain6.xyz`). |
| `fields` | Comma‑separated list of fields to return; here we only want `object_id` and `context`. |
| `limit` | Caps the number of records returned (8). |
| `key_def` | When set to `true`, the API includes the internal key definition (e.g., the field names) in the response header or body, depending on the provider’s implementation. |

If your provider uses a different query syntax (e.g., GraphQL, OData, or a custom REST shape), just adapt the `params` section accordingly. The key idea is to request only the two fields you care about, limit the result set, and enable the `key_def` flag.
